# Basics
1. "Everything in linux is a process"
-It starts with PID 1 (systemd)
   where, 'd' stands for daemon(background)

2. "Everything in Linux is a file/directory."
3. "Everything in Linux starts from '/'or root directory."

# basic commands  
1. whoami- username
2. pwd - present working directory
3. cd- change directory
4. ls- list files & directories
   ls -l - to see details of files(permisssions,size, users, groups)
5. cat- open file content 
6. echo- to print something
7. touch- to make file
8. mkdir- to make a directory
9. which- location of file
10. df -h - disk status
11. free -m - RAM status
12. head- to see the top lines
13. tail- to see the bottom lines
14. vim - text editor
15. htop/top- real time system resource usage 
16. nohup- to run a script in the background
17. wc -l - count how many lines are in a file

## User management (add sudo before every command of user management)
1. su- to switch users
2. sudo- to use root user permissions
3. etc/sudoers- file that contains superusers.
4. useradd - to add a user, but neither directory of user will be generated in home folder nor its password be set.
5. useradd -m - to add a user as well as create a directory for the user, but we have to set the password manually
6. sudo password- to set the password for the user
*7. adduser- to add a user, create a directory, set password
8. userdel- to remove group
9. cat /etc/passwd- to see all users
10. cat /etc/group- to see all groups
11. groupadd- to make a group
12. gpasswd- to add a user
            >> adding 1 user: [sudo gpasswd -a username groupname]
            >> adding multiple users: [sudo gpasswd -m user1,user2..... groupname] 
13. groupdel- to delete a group            
